Large and aligned allocations. frigg's slab_pool rounded a large length
up to pages and added its padding unchecked, so malloc, realloc and
posix_memalign of a size within some 260 KiB of SIZE_MAX wrapped to a
tiny mapping that was handed out as a block of the size asked; anything
above PTRDIFF_MAX now fails with ENOMEM, as in glibc and musl.
posix_memalign allocated max(size, alignment) and asserted that the
block came out aligned, but large blocks sit one page past a header at
the start of a 256 KiB unit, so any alignment above 4 KiB of a block
above 32 KiB aborted the program (posix_memalign(&p, 8192, 65536),
aligned_alloc(65536, 65536), and Rust's System allocator for any such
Layout). slab_pool has allocate_aligned now, which places a large block
on its alignment, with its header where free, realloc and
malloc_usable_size look for it; small ones are aligned by their bucket
as before. aligned_alloc takes any size, as C17 and glibc do (C11 first
asked for a multiple of the alignment), and fails only for an alignment
that is not a power of two (EINVAL). memalign is glibc's: an alignment is
rounded up to a power of two, past the largest one it is EINVAL; it was
"Not implemented" and aborted. build.sh and meson.build install the
Linux option's <malloc.h>, which programs and ports include. Meant for
upstream, in frigg and mlibc.

